    "recommendations": "Reducing brush directly adjacent to the structure and creating greater separation between shrubs, tree clusters, and the building perimeter could meaningfully improve defensible space and reduce fuel continuity around the improvements. Where dense shrub cover and mixed vegetation connect from the surrounding brushland toward the structure, thinning and separating those fuels may reduce the opportunity for fire to carry close to the building. Maintaining spacing between individual shrubs and between shrubs and any overhanging tree canopy would further support fuel continuity reduction across the property. Given the single access route serving the property, keeping that corridor clear of encroaching vegetation on both sides would support firefighter access and improve conditions for suppression operations. Preserving and expanding any open terrain near the structure as a buffer zone, and continuing to manage vegetation concentrations within the immediate structure perimeter, would support long-term wildfire resilience and improve overall property preparedness at this wildland interface location."
